%[text] ## K-Means Clustering
%[text] ### K-Means Clustering: Theory
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_3830] We need to perform clustering to identify "centers or centroids" representative of various colors or intensities in an image. The [k-means clustering](internal:M_9a81) algorithm accepts a user-chosen value of *k*, the number of clusters. Initially, it randomly selects the centroid locations for each cluster. Then, it assigns each data point to the nearest cluster based on the sum of squared distances (SSD) from the centroids. Due to the random selection of centroids, cluster assignments can vary.
%[text] The best cluster centers minimize the SSD between all points and their nearest cluster center, $c\_i${"altText":"c\_i"}, using the equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\nSSD = \\sum\_\\text{cluster i} \\sum\_{x \\in \\text{cluster i}} (x - c\_i)^2${"altText":" SSD = \sum\_\text{cluster i} \sum\_{x \in \text{cluster i}} (x - c\_i)^2"}
%[text] Manually determining optimal cluster centers is challenging because we don't know which pixels belong together. **K-means clustering** automates this process by iteratively refining the centroids to minimize the SSD, effectively grouping similar pixels. This is crucial for tasks like image segmentation, where accurately identifying regions with similar colors or intensities is essential.
%[text] ![](text:image:6e73) **Try**. Perform k-means clustering to segment the noisy toy image. Select the number of clusters, *k*.
  %[control:button:9c89]{"position":[1,2]}
fig = figure;
fig.Units = "pixels";
screensize = get(0,"ScreenSize");
gcf.Position = screensize;

tiledlayout(1,2)
nexttile(1)
ToyK = imread("Toy.jpg"); % Load image
ImgKmeans = imnoise(ToyK,"gaussian",0.3); %#ok<*NASGU>
ImgKmeans = rgb2lightness(ImgKmeans);

imshow(ImgKmeans,[])
title("Original Image")
% Convert to double for processing
ImgDouble = im2double(ImgKmeans);

K = 4; %[control:slider:0f4b]{"position":[5,6]}
nexttile(2)
[clusterIndices,score] = ScatKmeans(ImgDouble,K);
%%
%[text] #### Validation with Silhouette Plots
%[text] A silhouette graph is a useful tool for assessing the quality of clusters created by the **k-means clustering** algorithm. Silhouette graphs work by calculating a silhouette score for each data point, which ranges from -1 to 1, with higher scores indicating better clustering. The graph plots these scores on the x-axis and lists the data points by cluster on the y-axis. High silhouette scores indicate well-clustered points, while low or negative scores suggest points that might be incorrectly clustered or positioned between clusters. The width of each cluster in the graph represents the number of points within that cluster. If many points have low or negative scores, it may be necessary to adjust the number of clusters and re-run the k-means clustering algorithm.
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_858a] <u>Key criteria for good silhouette plots include</u>:
%[text] - ***Silhouette Scores***: High average scores indicate well-defined clusters.
%[text] - ***Separation***: Clear gaps between clusters, showing minimal overlap.
%[text] - ***Width***: Uniform widths within each cluster, representing the number of points. \
%[text] ![](text:image:04fe) **Try**. Adjust the k-value in the k-means algorithm to achieve the optimal silhouette graph. Use the key criteria for good silhouette plots to adjust your k-value.
K = 4; %[control:slider:7815]{"position":[5,6]}

% Create silhouette plot to compare the results of cluster data
figure
if exist("ImgDouble","var")
    tiledlayout(1,2)
    nexttile(1)
    [clusterIndices,score] = ScatKmeans(ImgDouble,K);

    nexttile(2)
    silhouette(score,clusterIndices,"cityblock");
    title("Silhouette Plot for K-means Clustering");
    xlabel("Silhouette Value")
    ylabel("Cluster Index")
    AvgSilVal = mean(clusterIndices);
    AvgSilVal = round(AvgSilVal,3,"significant");
    disp("The average silhouette value is: " + AvgSilVal)

    % Scale figure to screen size
    fig = gcf; % Get current figure
    screensize = get(0,"ScreenSize"); % Get screen size
    fig.Position = screensize; % Set figure position to full screen
else
    warning("Perform k-means clustering above.")
end
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:4199) **Exercise 2.** How many clusters should you use to segment the noisy toy image?
%[text] **Solution:**
CheckAnswer("Exercise2","Select") % Non-default options include: "Two", "Three", "Four", and "Five" %[control:dropdown:801e]{"position":[25,33]}
%%
%[text] ### K-Means Clustering: Applications
%[text] We've been working with grayscale images, but most of our images are in the RGB color space. RGB does not match human vision well. Using the L\*a\*b\* color space helps us see color differences better because it aligns more closely with human vision. L\*a\*b\* separates lightness (L\*) from color (a\* and b\*), allowing us to adjust brightness and color independently.
%[text] To segment by color, we convert the image to L\*a\*b\* and use k-means clustering to see the color-based groups. Each group represents similar pixels.
%[text] ![](text:image:790c) **Try**. Perform k-means clustering on an image.
%[text] 1. *Select an image on which to perform k-means clustering.*
%[text] 2. *(Optional) Check the box to preview the image.*
%[text] 3. *Select the number of clusters, k, to perform k-means clustering on your chosen image.*
%[text] 4. *Press the button to perform k-means clustering.* \
ImageR = "Select"; % Non-default options include: "Beach.jpg", "Glacier.jpg" and, "Scene.jpg" %[control:dropdown:6664]{"position":[10,18]}
if ~strcmp(ImageR,"Select")
    PrevImg = false; %[control:checkbox:33b2]{"position":[15,20]}
    k = 3; %[control:slider:7df8]{"position":[9,10]}
    [PixSamp,idx] = PlotKClust(ImageR,k,PrevImg);
      %[control:button:5925]{"position":[5,6]}
else
    warning("Select a valid option.")
    return
end
%%
%[text] #### Validation of K-means Clustering
%[text] Remember the key criteria for good silhouette plots. These criteria suggest that the clustering structure is strong and that the chosen *k* effectively captures the natural grouping in the data. If the silhouette plot meets these criteria, it means you have likely chosen a value of *k* that is close to the correct number of clusters for your data.
%[text] ![](text:image:4fdc) **Try**. Evaluate your choice of k used to perform k-means clustering on your chosen image above.
if exist("ImageR","var")
    k = 2; %[control:slider:0ef0]{"position":[9,10]}
    [PixSamp,idx] = PlotKClust(ImageR,k,false);
      %[control:button:266e]{"position":[5,6]}

    % Create silhouette graph for the clustering (only if clusters are valid)
    if numel(unique(idx)) > 1
        figure
        silhouette(PixSamp,idx,"cityblock");
        title("Silhouette Plot for K-means Clustering");
        xlabel("Silhouette Value")
        ylabel("Cluster Index")
    else
        warning("Silhouette plot cannot be generated with only one cluster.");
    end
    AvgSilValB = mean(idx);
    AvgSilValB = round(AvgSilValB,3,"significant");
    disp("The average silhouette value is: " + AvgSilValB)
else
    warning("Select an image above.")
end

